## React version

19.2.7 (also present on `main` in `packages/react-dom-bindings/src/server/fizz-instruction-set/ReactDOMFizzInstructionSetShared.js`)

## Summary

When an SSR-streamed page is loaded in a **hidden tab** (opened via cmd/ctrl+click, a restored background tab, or an automation-driven tab), streamed Suspense boundaries are never revealed: the completion segments arrive and sit in the DOM, `document.readyState` becomes `"complete"`, but the user-visible DOM shows the fallbacks indefinitely — until the tab becomes visible. React also does not client-render these boundaries, so nothing inside the page can recover on its own.

## Mechanism

In `completeBoundary` (`ReactDOMFizzInstructionSetShared.js`):

1. The boundary is marked queued — comment data set to `$~` (`SUSPENSE_QUEUED_START_DATA`) — *"so we know not to client render it at the end of document load"*, and the pair is pushed to `window.$RB`.
2. On the **first** push (`$RB.length === 2`), since `window.$RT` is not yet a number, the flush is scheduled with:

   ```js
   // If we haven't had our rAF callback yet, schedule everything for the first paint.
   requestAnimationFrame(window['$RV'].bind(null, window['$RB']));
   ```

3. Per spec, `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks do not run while the document is hidden — so `$RV` never runs and `$RT` is never set.
4. Every subsequent `completeBoundary` call pushes onto the still-unflushed `$RB` (`length > 2`, so no new scheduling happens). The `setTimeout` throttle path (which would fire even in hidden tabs) is only reachable once `$RT` has been set by a first flush.
5. On the hydration side, `isSuspenseInstancePending` treats `$~` as pending, so the client never falls back to client-rendering these boundaries at end of document load.

Net effect: a deadlock for the lifetime of the hidden state. The whole page's content is present in hidden `<div hidden id="S:n">` segments, but the visible DOM is fallbacks only.

## Observed evidence (production build, Next.js 16.2.9 / react-dom 19.2.7)

On a hidden tab, ~50 s after load: `document.visibilityState === "hidden"`, `window.$RB.length === 4` (two queued boundaries), `window.$RT === undefined`, boundary comments are `$~`, fallbacks still visible. Calling `window.$RV(window.$RB)` manually from the console reveals everything instantly and removes the completion segments.

## Steps to reproduce

```js
// server.js — npm i react react-dom, node server.js
const http = require('http');
const { createElement: h, Suspense } = require('react');
const { renderToPipeableStream } = require('react-dom/server');

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url !== '/') { res.statusCode = 404; return res.end(); }
  let done = false, promise = null;
  function Slow() {
    if (!done) {
      promise ??= new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => { done = true; r(); }, 1000));
      throw promise;
    }
    return h('h1', null, 'Revealed');
  }
  const app = h('html', null, h('body', null,
    h(Suspense, { fallback: h('p', null, 'Loading…') }, h(Slow))
  ));
  res.setHeader('content-type', 'text/html');
  const { pipe } = renderToPipeableStream(app, { onShellReady() { pipe(res); } });
}).listen(3333);
```

1. `node server.js`
2. cmd/ctrl+click `http://localhost:3333` so it opens in a **background** tab, and leave it in the background.
3. Wait any amount of time; the tab keeps showing “Loading…” (inspect from DevTools/CDP: the `S:` segment is in the DOM, `$RB` is non-empty, `$RT` undefined).
4. Focus the tab → the pending rAF fires and the content appears.

## Expected behavior

Streamed boundaries should (eventually) reveal even while the document is hidden — e.g. schedule the first flush with a `setTimeout` fallback alongside the rAF, or flush on `visibilitychange`. Hidden documents are still read by automation/E2E harnesses, accessibility tooling, extensions, and analytics; and for users, work queued behind rAF means the tab they preloaded in the background reveals only after they switch to it (late LCP / skeleton flash instead of a ready page).

If deferring reveals in hidden tabs is intentional (paint-related batching), it would be good to have that documented — and to reconsider marking the boundary `$~` before a flush is actually guaranteed to run, since `$~` is precisely what prevents the client-render fallback from ever rescuing the boundary.
